WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.580
today we're going to be speaking with Frank
von Hippel, who is a professor at Northern

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.740
Arizona University and author of the book
The Chemical Age How Chemists Fought Famine

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:15.420
and Disease, Killed Millions and Changed Our
Relationship with the Earth.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:22.060
And Frank, you know, in reading about the
history of chemistry and chemists, it seems

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:28.609
that a theme with with essentially every technological
advancement in human history, agriculture

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:31.900
comes to mind, nuclear power comes to mind.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:40.079
We both have much good and the possibility
of much bad that I am I am I correctly understanding

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:43.130
that that is essentially the broad history
of chemistry?

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.230
Yes, for sure.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:48.910
And like you're saying, it's also the history
with physics and many other fields that lead

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:53.460
to technological advances, that we have these
wonderful innovations, but they have massive

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.629
unintended consequences.

00:00:55.629 --> 00:01:01.070
I think a lot of times when people think about
chemistry, they think about the last hundred

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:06.470
years, 150 years, maybe two hundred years,
and they think of chemicals that include things

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:12.720
like pesticides as an example or chemistry
used in medical technologies.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.030
But the history of chemistry really goes back
much further than that.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.030
It does.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.350
It really goes back to alchemy in a way where
people were using trial and error to find

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.610
new ways of doing things often impossible,
things like trying to turn lead into gold.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:30.350
But those experiments led to a lot of advancements.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:35.070
And I would say that modern chemistry really
arose in the early eighteen hundreds with

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:39.890
the advent of organic chemistry as chemists
learned how to synthesize different kinds

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:43.020
of chemicals that are only otherwise found
in nature.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.560
And that's what gave rise to our modern pesticides,
our modern chemical weapons and so on.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:53.240
It seems that correct me if I'm wrong, but
there's a bit of an analogous history with

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:59.310
what what we now know of as two very different
fields, astronomy and astrology.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:03.990
At one point there was an overlap between
those and then they eventually sort of separated

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.490
and much the same way.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:11.129
Alchemy, which now is the subject of many
sort of pseudo science books and and sometimes

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:13.980
overlaps with everything from psychics and
other things.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.459
At one point it was right there at the start
of modern chemistry as well.

00:02:18.459 --> 00:02:19.459
That's exactly right.

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:25.209
And so with the advent of modern science where
we disprove things and we subject ideas to

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:30.579
experimentation, of course alchemy went away
and we have the the modern field of chemistry

00:02:30.579 --> 00:02:32.579
rise out of that.

00:02:32.579 --> 00:02:38.359
When we start to think back in the history
of chemistry and thinking about the major

00:02:38.359 --> 00:02:44.780
breakthroughs within the field, what are sort
of like the top level, most important milestones?

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:50.349
Well, I would say the first one would be the
synthesis of urea by Frederic Vogeler in eighteen

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:51.349
twenty eight.

00:02:51.349 --> 00:02:54.549
And that's really the the point of origin
of organic chemistry.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:57.060
But there are many others after that.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.959
And in terms of the history of pesticides,
we have the first commercial pesticide which

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:07.780
was called Bordeaux mixture as a copper sulfate
solution in the eighteen seventies, and that

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:13.920
then led to the proliferation of initially
toxic metal based pesticides.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.209
And then we had major inflection points in
World War One and World War two, World War

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:23.560
One, because it was incredible cross-fertilization
of talent of chemists who were working both

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.120
on pesticides and chemical weapons.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.989
So we had pesticides become a chemical weapons
and we had chemical weapons become pesticides.

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:35.330
And by the end of World War One, a quarter
of the artillery shells flying over the trenches

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:38.510
had chemical weapons in them, which is extraordinary
to think about.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.700
Now, just that was really a chemical war.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.849
It was the chemists who who really decided
the outcome of that war.

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:50.840
And then things got even more advanced and
and horrific in a way, with World War Two,

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:56.150
where we got the advent of the organophosphate
nerve gases like sarin and tavan, which were

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:00.989
developed by Nazi scientists and those developed
into the modern organophosphate insecticides

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:02.560
after the war.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.219
So early on in the history of pesticides.

00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:12.269
I mean, my reading of it seems to suggest
that this was really seen as just a boon and

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:18.800
a great thing, all positives in the pros column
and really nothing in the cons because it

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.710
allowed more food to get to the point at which
it could provide sustenance to people and

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:28.000
it provided an expansion of available calories
and really solved a lot of problems that were

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.740
that were prevalent at the time.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.940
Was it that were there people sounding alarms
early on and sort of like what is the history

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.720
of when the other side of pesticides started
to become more known?

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:44.140
Yeah, it's a terrific question because initially
chemists who were developing these chemicals

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:49.580
were fighting the scourges of humanity that
have faced us since we started agriculture.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:52.930
They were fighting famine and they were fighting
infectious diseases.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:05:00.890
So famine, because crops are destroyed by
pests like rats and cockroaches and grasshoppers

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:03.790
and so on, but also crops are destroyed by
plant pathogens.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:10.330
And that's what led to the potato blight disaster
that caused the greatest famine ever recorded

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:11.800
in Ireland in the 1840.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:16.230
So chemists were trying to fight famine and
they were also trying to fight infectious

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:21.480
diseases that were vectored by animals like
malaria and yellow fever, typhus, bubonic

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.300
plague and so on.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:27.830
And so the motivations were really good and
these chemicals were quite effective.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:31.790
They were averting famine and averting diseases.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:36.370
But the first alarms really started, I would
say, right after World War One.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:41.940
There were a lot of people getting poisoned
by toxic metals in their food, especially

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:47.060
lead and arsenic, which were used in insecticides
and often present on produce.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:50.890
And if people didn't wash the produce, they
could get poisoned from it.

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.100
But even then, it was kind of a grumbling.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:57.400
And then after World War Two, there was further
grumbling as people saw widespread loss of

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:58.540
wildlife.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:04.390
It really wasn't until 1962 when Rachel Carson
Popish Silent Spring that we saw massive public

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:09.580
outrage about the pollution and the destruction
of the environment caused by these chemicals,

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:14.960
as well as human health effects when it comes
to there's now sort of a new sort of newer

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.660
movement of earth friendly.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:24.040
And again, it's this idea of of so-called
pesticides, but they're sort of like non chemical

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.790
or friendly things.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:28.400
I'm not actually I'm not actually aware of
what the substances are.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:33.310
But you'll sometimes hear, hey, you know,
you can use you spray lavender on your garden.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:38.530
This is not a real example, but you put lavender
on your garden and then put onion peels and

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.930
then the animals just don't come near.

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:46.080
Is is there what is the chemistry community
think about that type of thing?

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:52.980
Can something totally innocuous really be
as effective as some of these more pernicious

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:58.250
chemical pesticides, or is that in order to
get the huge benefit, you've got the huge

00:06:58.250 --> 00:06:59.250
risk as well?

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.970
Yeah, so it's a great question.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:05.250
And unfortunately, with the big benefit of
using chemical pesticides, we also create

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:10.970
our own dependency on them because these chemicals
not only kill the pests, they kill the predators

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:12.710
and parasites of those pests.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.990
So they kill the spiders that eat those pests.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:20.140
They kill the the parasitic dwarfs that in
fact, those pests, they kill the birds that

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:21.770
eat those pests.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:26.400
And then you end up in this vicious cycle
where you have to use more and more chemicals

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.210
to get the same yield.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:31.150
So it's not a simple answer and.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:35.170
And really, there are many organic solutions
that don't require chemical pesticides that

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:40.400
are quite effective, but part of it has to
do with the way crops are rotated, grow in

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.780
a diversity of crops rather than a monoculture.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:47.310
If you have ten thousand acres of the same
crop, of course, you're going to get an outbreak

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:51.150
of pests because it's this giant salad bowl
for them.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:55.920
So we need to be thinking holistically about
how we grow our food and at the same time

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.070
reserve chemicals for when we really need
them.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:02.640
In my opinion, that would be for fighting
infectious diseases where we we have outbreaks

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.440
of pests that we need to handle.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:10.950
Yeah, I found absolutely fascinating Omnivore's
Dilemma by by Michael Pollan, where he talks

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:17.300
about going to Joel Salatin farm and I believe
it's Virginia and explains how there's this

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:24.000
kind of vicious cycle of, you know, you start
to need, for example, antibiotics for cows

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.080
when the cows are being fed grain instead
of grass, because that's not natural for the

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:31.370
digestive system of the cow.

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:37.349
And it at least makes the case that at some
level you can have this regenerative agriculture.

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:42.459
That doesn't depend on the types of pesticides
that that you write about in the book.

00:08:42.459 --> 00:08:46.041
Can that can that really work at scale?

00:08:46.041 --> 00:08:50.381
Yeah, so I think it's a great question that
we need to explore as a society, because it

00:08:50.381 --> 00:08:52.199
hasn't really been tried.

00:08:52.199 --> 00:08:57.620
We went from operating a small farms throughout
the world to having industrialized agriculture

00:08:57.620 --> 00:08:59.209
after World War Two.

00:08:59.209 --> 00:09:02.040
Population has grown tremendously.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.910
And now we need to see can we do a sustainably?

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:08.160
We have to find a way to do sustainably because
it's certainly not sustainable the way that

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.140
we rely on chemicals now and the damage it
does to our health and the environment.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:17.220
So there's going to be some kind of a compromise
where we're using the minimum of these pesticides

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:23.480
that that one needs to use while maximizing
all of these other benefits of using diverse

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:28.120
agriculture and and and going to a smaller
scale, more local markets.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:31.980
Why are we shipping foods all around the world
that can be grown nearby?

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:38.110
So we need to completely rethink our agriculture
up to go back a little bit to the sort of

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:42.870
public health side of of the field of chemistry.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:43.870
What do you make generally?

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:48.439
I mean, I have more specific questions, but
what do you make generally about the types

00:09:48.439 --> 00:09:55.560
of concerns expressed over the last few months
about the coming coronavirus vaccines, some

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:01.519
based on the speed with which the vaccines
were developed, some based more on conspiracy

00:10:01.519 --> 00:10:06.029
theories about Bill Gates and microchips,
but concerns that at least some some seem

00:10:06.029 --> 00:10:11.640
in good faith about the makeup of the vaccines
and the storage and the long term effect on

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:12.870
the body.

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:16.339
What's your general sense of the discussion
around vaccination right now?

00:10:16.339 --> 00:10:17.339
Right.

00:10:17.339 --> 00:10:22.080
So I'm a big advocate of vaccines and including
the new vaccines coming out for covid.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.360
I think they're incredibly important.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:30.120
And although the vaccine for covid-19 was
developed incredibly quickly over the last

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:34.579
10 months or so, the technology it's based
on has been in development for 20 years.

00:10:34.579 --> 00:10:39.730
And so it's not it's a new technology to be
employed for a human vaccine, but it's been

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:43.959
in development for a long time and the biology
is well understood.

00:10:43.959 --> 00:10:48.100
So to me, the vaccine looks very safe, it
looks efficacious.

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:51.610
It's more efficacious than many of us were
expecting.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:55.749
And certainly I'll be asking my parents to
get it as soon as it's available for older

00:10:55.749 --> 00:10:58.529
people and for the rest of us when it's available.

00:10:58.529 --> 00:11:05.300
So and as far as the D the slander against
Bill Gates and people like him is just horrible.

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:10.089
Here you have someone who's working very hard
for the public benefit, using his own resources

00:11:10.089 --> 00:11:12.670
to do so, and then he's getting attacked for
it.

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:16.399
I just find it absolutely horrifying that
that's going on.

00:11:16.399 --> 00:11:20.059
One of the arguments that sometimes being
made and to call it an argument maybe is a

00:11:20.059 --> 00:11:21.059
bit of a stretch.

00:11:21.059 --> 00:11:27.819
But one of the claims that's being made is
that we don't know if by test, by testing

00:11:27.819 --> 00:11:32.660
these vaccines for a few months on people
in phase three trials, we don't know if a

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:38.059
year, two, three, four years down the line,
there will be some unforeseen follow on effects.

00:11:38.059 --> 00:11:42.680
Now, of course, it's a sort of indefeasible
argument in the sense that if a vaccine has

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:47.560
been around 50 years, you don't really know
that after fifty one, it doesn't cause some

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:48.560
new problems.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:50.059
So you could always make this argument.

00:11:50.059 --> 00:11:54.439
But in terms of what we know about vaccines,
what the only thing I've been able to find

00:11:54.439 --> 00:12:01.319
about long term vaccine effects that go beyond
a few days is a vaccine, the exact one I forget

00:12:01.319 --> 00:12:06.139
that doubled the risk of ironbar syndrome,
I think from one in two million to one in

00:12:06.139 --> 00:12:10.119
one million over the life of the individual
up.

00:12:10.119 --> 00:12:15.149
What can we say about, generally speaking,
at the chemical level, the idea that years

00:12:15.149 --> 00:12:18.130
down the line a vaccine could cause something?

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:19.130
Sure.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:23.319
And actually the example you gave is a good
one, because typically where vaccines have

00:12:23.319 --> 00:12:27.920
an adverse effect is that the scale of one
in a million or two in a million people that

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:30.839
have some serious consequence of that.

00:12:30.839 --> 00:12:35.959
And if you compare that to the serious consequence
of getting measles or getting mumps and rubella

00:12:35.959 --> 00:12:41.769
or or tetanus or any of these other things
that we get vaccinated against, it's it's

00:12:41.769 --> 00:12:42.769
there's no comparison.

00:12:42.769 --> 00:12:47.100
So, yes, there may be one in a million, two
in a million people who, due to their genetic

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:51.850
makeup and their environment, have some serious
consequence of a new vaccine.

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:55.619
But look at the mortality rate of people from
covid-19.

00:12:55.619 --> 00:13:00.509
And we have about one percent or almost one
percent of people infected are dying.

00:13:00.509 --> 00:13:05.050
And that scale there's no comparison to one
in a million versus one in one hundred.

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:11.069
So in terms of vaccine history, I think that's
what the lesson is, is that nearly always

00:13:11.069 --> 00:13:12.209
they proved to be safe.

00:13:12.209 --> 00:13:16.879
There are cases where they're not safe, usually
discover that quite quickly.

00:13:16.879 --> 00:13:23.519
And if this happened with a dengue fever vaccine,
for example, and so when those problems occur,

00:13:23.519 --> 00:13:25.480
they're pulled off the market, the problem
is solved.

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:29.540
And we're very lucky to live in a time where
the science is so advanced that something

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:33.850
like this can't be developed this quickly
rather than suffering through 30 years of

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:37.710
covid-19 before we have a solution.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:38.710
Very, very well said.

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:43.490
We've been speaking with Frank von Hippel,
who teaches at Northern Arizona University.

00:13:43.490 --> 00:13:45.040
The book is The Chemical.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:49.800
Age, how chemists fought famine and disease,
killed millions and changed our relationship

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:50.800
with the Earth.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:53.230
A Frank, I so appreciate your time today.

